        pull(c)\n\nBut we need to ensure that after cut, the parity of nodes in c subtree relative to its new root is correct. Using parity_rev lazy works.\n\nNow we need to answer queries:\n\nIf same component: lca != 0.\n\nCompute parity = parity[u] ^ parity[v] (after ensuring both are in same tree). If odd flag of component (odd[root]) is True: output CONTRADICTION.\n\nElse (bipartite): if parity == 0: SAME else DIFFERENT.\n\nIf not same component: DISCONNECTED.\n\nEdge cases: When component is non-bipartite, we output CONTRADICTION regardless of parity.\n\nImplementation details: Need function root(x): while fa[x]!=-1: x=fa[x]; return x.\n\nAfter evert, root's parity = 0.\n\nNow we need to process input lines. Input format: first line n q. Then q lines each starting with '+', '-', '?' followed by u v.\n\nWe'll read sys.stdin.read().split().\n\nImplement LCT class with methods.\n\nComplexities O((n+q) log n). Should fit.\n\nTesting with sample.\n\nPotential pitfalls: When linking, we need to ensure we don't link nodes already in same component (guaranteed not). When cutting, we need to ensure edge exists (guaranteed). Our cut algorithm expects direct child relationship; after series of adds/removes, the edge we cut may not be direct child because we may have performed other link/cut operations causing restructuring. However LCT maintains that the edge we cut is a direct child of its parent in the splay tree representation after access. To cut, we can perform access(u) then access(v) then cut between v and its parent. Standard LCT cut procedure: access(u); access(v); cut(v, parent(v)). Implementation: after access(v), v is root of its tree, and its parent is the edge we want to cut. So we can cut by removing child pointer from parent to v.\n\nThus cut operation should be:\n\ndef cut(u, v):\n    access(u)\n    access(v)\n    # now v is root, its parent is the edge to cut\n    p = fa[v]\n    if p != -1:\n        # remove child pointer\n        ch[p][ch[p][0]==v] = -